[claude] 🔧 mcp__kernelgen__finalize_round: {"conclusion": {"expectation_status": "partially_met", "knowledge_assessments": [], "next_suggestion": "Attack the device side for the three 4096x4096 workloads: keep the validated fp32-staged compare/select kernel for float32 and general (non-saturating) values, but for 2-byte float dtypes in the saturating case lower the infinity handling to max(min(x,pos),neg) in the input dtype and raise the tile to 16384 elements (32 KiB staging cap).", "optimization_level": "host_side_special", "perf_gap_analysis": "Frozen expectation: bypassing the JIT binder cuts ~55 us of per-call host cost so every workload converges on max(host launch rate, device time), raising geo_mean. Observed: tiny cases fell from 72.0-78.0 us to 19.0-20.0 us (-73%) and geo_mean rose from 0.208 to 0.748, so the host-overhead mechanism is confirmed. Gap: the same fixed cost was not the whole story for the large workloads - their latency did not move at all (timing-3 83.51 -> 83.44 us, timing-7 85.22 -> 85.12 us) and timing-11 regressed 84.21 -> 88.72 us, which proves those three cases are device-bound, not dispatch-bound. A bounded debug job (MEASURED on target, staging kernel = 84.4 us at 4096x4096 float16) confirmed that the device time is real and that the tile size, not the host path, is what limits it (native-dtype clamp with a 16384-element tile measured 58.5 us). My round-1 inference that the large cases were mostly host overhead was wrong because it came from subtracting two independent measurements.", "root_cause": "Per-call Triton JIT dispatch was the dominant cost for small tensors and removing it delivered a 3.6x geo_mean gain; the remaining 4096x4096 cases are limited on the device by per-program/tile granularity (2048 programs of 16 KiB staging tiles) rather than by the launch path.", "round_num": 2}}
